# Batch 36 (2026-11-23): deep time and human origins; South and Southeast Asia; Oceania and the Americas; Africa
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: deep time and human origins
E+=[
X('late-ordovician-extinction','The Late Ordovician mass extinction: the first of the "big five"',H,'confirmed',2,-445000000,['geological','fossil','geochemical'],
 "About 445 million years ago, when almost all animal life lived in the sea, the Late Ordovician mass extinction wiped out an estimated 49-60% of marine genera and close to 85% of marine species, making it the first of the 'big five' mass extinctions and usually ranked second only to the end-Permian 'Great Dying'. Trilobites, brachiopods, bryozoans, graptolites, conodonts and corals were all hit. It came in two main pulses: the first is generally tied to a sudden glaciation over the southern supercontinent Gondwana, which cooled the climate and drained shallow seas as sea level fell; the second, as the ice melted, is linked to widespread anoxia (loss of oxygen) and toxic sulphide-rich water. Diversity recovered over roughly the first 5 million years of the Silurian.",
 [('Gamma-ray burst','In 2004 Adrian Melott and colleagues proposed that a gamma-ray burst from a nearby exploding star could have stripped away much of the ozone layer and triggered the cooling; this is a published but untested hypothesis, as such an event would leave little direct geological trace.'),
  ('Volcanism and weathering','Other researchers emphasise volcanic activity and the weathering of new mountain ranges, which can draw carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere and help explain why a greenhouse world flipped so quickly into an ice age; the relative weight of each cause is still debated.')],
 ['doi:10.1017/S1473550404001910','wiki:Late_Ordovician_mass_extinction'],img='Late Ordovician mass extinction',dateNote='About 445-443 million years ago, in two or more pulses'),
X('burgess-shale','The Burgess Shale: weird wonders of the Cambrian seas',H,'confirmed',2,-508000000,['fossil','geological'],
 "The Burgess Shale is a black shale in the Canadian Rockies of British Columbia, about 508-505 million years old, famous for preserving soft-bodied animals of the Cambrian explosion as thin carbon films. Charles Walcott of the Smithsonian Institution discovered it on 30 August 1909 and collected tens of thousands of specimens, trying to fit them into living animal groups. From the 1960s Harry Whittington, Derek Briggs and Simon Conway Morris re-examined the fossils and found many that did not fit modern groups comfortably, such as five-eyed Opabinia and Hallucigenia, which was first reconstructed upside down and back to front. The site is part of the Canadian Rocky Mountain Parks UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 [('Gould versus Conway Morris','In his 1989 book Wonderful Life, Stephen Jay Gould argued that the Burgess animals showed far greater disparity of body plans than survives today and that which lineages survived was largely down to chance; Conway Morris, whose work Gould relied on, strongly disagreed, arguing that most Cambrian animals belong in or near modern phyla and that evolution tends to converge on similar solutions.'),
  ('Creationist readings','Some creationist writers cite the sudden appearance of complex Cambrian animals as evidence against gradual evolution; palaeontologists point to older Ediacaran and early Cambrian fossils and to molecular evidence that animal lineages began diverging earlier.')],
 ['wiki:Burgess_Shale','wiki:Hallucigenia','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/304|Canadian Rocky Mountain Parks (UNESCO World Heritage List)|heritage listing'],img='Burgess Shale',dateNote='Rocks about 508-505 million years old; discovered 1909',
 changed="Walcott (1909-1927) placed the animals in known groups; Whittington, Briggs and Conway Morris (from the late 1960s) showed many were stranger than thought; Gould popularised them in 1989; later finds, including Chinese and Canadian sites, moved many 'weird wonders' back towards the stem groups of living phyla."),
X('archaeopteryx','Archaeopteryx: the "first bird" and the claim it was forged',H,'confirmed',2,-150000000,['fossil'],
 "Archaeopteryx is a magpie-to-raven-sized feathered dinosaur from the Late Jurassic, about 150 million years ago, found in the limestones of southern Germany. It was named from a single feather in 1861, only two years after Charles Darwin's On the Origin of Species, and the first skeleton (the London specimen) was bought by the British Museum the following year. With teeth, clawed fingers and a long bony tail combined with flight feathers and wings, it became a classic transitional fossil between non-avian dinosaurs and birds. Around fourteen body fossils are now known. It was long called the oldest bird, although several possibly older bird-like dinosaurs have since been described, mainly from China.",
 [('Forgery claim (debunked)','From 1985 a group including astronomer Fred Hoyle and physicist Lee Spetner claimed the feather impressions on the London and Berlin specimens had been faked with a thin layer of cement. Natural History Museum scientists led by Alan Charig examined the specimens and published "Archaeopteryx is not a forgery" in Science (1986), showing among other things that hairline cracks pass through the feather impressions and match on both halves of the split slab; the claim is rejected by palaeontologists.'),
  ('Bird or dinosaur?','Whether Archaeopteryx sits at the base of the bird lineage or is better grouped with the deinonychosaurs (the raptor-like dinosaurs) has flipped in different analyses; this is a genuine scientific debate about classification, not about whether the fossils are real.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.232.4750.622','wiki:Archaeopteryx'],img='Archaeopteryx',dateNote='Lived about 150 million years ago; feather found 1861',statusNote='fossils genuine; forgery claim debunked'),
X('ardipithecus-ardi','Ardi (Ardipithecus ramidus): a 4.4-million-year-old ancestor unlike a chimpanzee',H,'confirmed',2,-4400000,['fossil','geological'],
 "Ardipithecus ramidus is an early hominin from Aramis in the Afar region of Ethiopia, dated to about 4.4 million years ago. The first remains were found in 1992-1993 by the Middle Awash research project and described in 1994 by Tim White, Gen Suwa and Berhane Asfaw. A partial female skeleton nicknamed 'Ardi' was published in a special issue of Science in 2009. Ardi combined a grasping, divergent big toe for climbing with features of the pelvis and foot suggesting she could also walk upright, and the species had small canine teeth in both sexes. Her discoverers argued that the last common ancestor of humans and chimpanzees did not closely resemble any living ape.",
 [('How upright, how close to us?','Some anthropologists question how bipedal Ardipithecus really was and whether it lies on the direct line to humans or on a side branch; the team that found it maintains that it is closely related to later Australopithecus.'),
  ('Social behaviour','The small canines have been read by Owen Lovejoy and others as a sign of less male-male fighting and possibly pair-bonding and more paternal care; this is an interpretation that other researchers regard as speculative.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.1175802','wiki:Ardipithecus_ramidus'],img='Ardipithecus ramidus',
 changed="Before 2009 many accounts assumed the earliest hominins looked much like chimpanzees; the Ardi publications argued that chimpanzees have themselves changed a great deal since the split."),
X('gigantopithecus','Gigantopithecus: the giant ape found among "dragon bones"',H,'confirmed',2,-2000000,['fossil','geological','luminescence/U-series dating'],
 "Gigantopithecus blacki was a huge extinct ape of southern China, known only from teeth and four lower jaws. In 1935 the anthropologist Ralph von Koenigswald identified its first molars in a Hong Kong drugstore, where fossil teeth were being sold as 'dragon bones' for traditional Chinese medicine. It is now thought to be related to orangutans rather than to humans. Size estimates of 200-300 kg are uncertain because so little of the skeleton is known. A 2024 study in Nature led by Yingqi Zhang and Kira Westaway dated its disappearance to between about 295,000 and 215,000 years ago and linked it to increasingly seasonal climate and the loss of the forest food it relied on.",
 [('Yeti and Bigfoot','Some cryptozoologists, notably the anthropologist Grover Krantz, proposed that surviving Gigantopithecus could explain reports of the Yeti or Bigfoot; no Gigantopithecus fossils are known outside mainland Asia or from recent times, and mainstream scientists regard the idea as unsupported.'),
  ('Humans as a factor','Earlier writers suggested that competition with or hunting by archaic humans helped finish it off; the 2024 study found no evidence that hominins were involved in the extinctions in southern China at that time, though absence of evidence there is not conclusive.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41586-023-06900-0','wiki:Gigantopithecus'],img='Gigantopithecus',end=-215000,dateNote='About 2 million to 295,000-215,000 years ago; identified 1935'),
X('acheulean-handaxes','Acheulean hand axes: a 1.7-million-year tool tradition and the discovery of human antiquity',H,'confirmed',2,-1760000,['archaeological','geological'],
 "The Acheulean is a stone-tool tradition defined by the hand axe, a teardrop-shaped tool shaped symmetrically on both faces. It is associated with Homo erectus and later species such as Homo heidelbergensis and spread across Africa, western and southern Asia and Europe. The oldest well-dated examples, from Kokiselei in West Turkana, Kenya, are about 1.76 million years old, and finds reported from Ethiopia in 2023 may be older still. The tradition lasted well over a million years. It is named after Saint-Acheul near Amiens in France, where hand axes were found in 1859.",
 [('Language and aesthetics','Some researchers argue that the care and symmetry of hand axes, sometimes beyond what was strictly useful, show planning, teaching and perhaps an aesthetic sense, and may be linked to the evolution of language; others see the symmetry as a by-product of efficient knapping.'),
  ('The Movius Line','In 1948 Hallam Movius drew a line across northern India, arguing that hand axes were missing east of it; later finds in Korea, China and Mongolia have cast doubt on how sharp that divide really was.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature10372','wiki:Acheulean'],img='Acheulean',end=-130000,dateNote='About 1.76 million to roughly 130,000 years ago',
 changed="In 1797 John Frere sent hand axes from Hoxne in Suffolk to London and suggested they were very old; Jacques Boucher de Perthes collected more in the Somme gravels in 1836-1846 but was widely disbelieved; in 1859 British geologists and antiquaries visited the Somme and accepted that humans had lived alongside extinct animals, the same year as Darwin's Origin."),
X('sahul-megafauna','Diprotodon and the vanished giants of Australia: hunting, fire or climate?',H,'disputed',2,-46000,['fossil','luminescence dating','isotopic','oral tradition'],
 "During the Pleistocene, Australia and New Guinea (together called Sahul) were home to giant animals including Diprotodon, the largest marsupial ever known, which was up to 4 m long and weighed perhaps as much as 3,500 kg. There was also a marsupial lion (Thylacoleo), giant kangaroos, the large flightless bird Genyornis and big reptiles. Diprotodon bones were first found in Wellington Caves, New South Wales, in 1830, and Richard Owen named the animal in 1838. A 2001 study by Richard Roberts and colleagues dated many megafaunal sites and concluded that the mainland megafauna disappeared around 46,000 years ago, after people had arrived. Some species may have lasted longer in places, and the timing is still argued over.",
 [('People did it','Many researchers, including Tim Flannery, argue that hunting and changes to the landscape caused by people using fire drove the extinctions; scorch marks on Genyornis eggshells have been read as direct evidence of humans cooking them, and megafauna in Tasmania seem to have vanished soon after a land bridge opened about 43,000 years ago.'),
  ('Climate did it, or both','Others argue that increasing aridity, or a combination of climate change and human pressure, was responsible, and question proxies such as dung-fungus spores used to time the declines.'),
  ('Memories in Dreamtime stories','From 1845 colonists recorded Aboriginal people identifying Diprotodon bones as bunyip remains, and some writers suggest that Dreamtime stories of giant creatures, such as the Kadimakara of Lake Eyre, preserve memories of megafauna; this is plausible but unconfirmed.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.1060264','wiki:Australian_megafauna','wiki:Diprotodon'],img='Diprotodon',dateNote='Most mainland megafauna gone by about 46,000-40,000 years ago (debated)',statusNote='extinctions confirmed; causes disputed'),
X('florisbad-skull','The Florisbad skull: a 259,000-year-old face from the Free State',H,'confirmed',3,-259000,['fossil','ESR dating'],
 "The Florisbad skull is a partial human cranium found in 1932 by the zoologist Thomas Frederik Dreyer in the deposits of a mineral spring at Florisbad, near Bloemfontein in the Free State, South Africa, together with animal fossils and stone tools. It was described in 1935. In 1996 Rainer Grün and colleagues dated a tooth from the skull directly using electron spin resonance to about 259,000 years ago, give or take about 35,000 years, making it one of the key fossils of the early Middle Stone Age. It is held at the National Museum in Bloemfontein.",
 [('Archaic or modern?','The skull is variously classified as late Homo heidelbergensis (or Homo helmei), or as an early member of Homo sapiens; it is central to the idea that our species emerged gradually across Africa, with fossils such as Jebel Irhoud in Morocco and Florisbad in the south, rather than in a single place.'),
  ('A pan-African origin','Some researchers see Florisbad, Jebel Irhoud and other fossils as evidence for a structured, continent-wide origin of modern humans; others favour a more localised origin with later spread. Both views are actively debated.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/382500a0','wiki:Florisbad_Skull'],img='Florisbad Skull',dateNote='About 259,000 ± 35,000 years old; found 1932'),
X('niah-deep-skull','Niah Caves and the "Deep Skull" of Borneo',H,'confirmed',3,-40000,['archaeological','fossil','radiocarbon'],
 "The Niah Caves in Sarawak, Malaysian Borneo, are one of the most important early human sites in Southeast Asia. Tom Harrisson, curator of the Sarawak Museum, began investigating them in 1954, and in February 1958 a team led by Barbara Harrisson found the 'Deep Skull', the skull of an adolescent modern human, in a trench nicknamed the 'Hell Trench'. Radiocarbon dates suggested an age of about 40,000 years. Doubts about the Harrissons' records and stratigraphy led to a new Niah Caves Project led by Graeme Barker in the 2000s, which reported in 2007 that modern humans had indeed lived and foraged in the lowland rainforest there at an early date. Neolithic burials 2,500-5,000 years old and later cave paintings and boat-shaped coffins are also known from Niah.",
 [('Who were they?','In 1960 Don Brothwell compared the Deep Skull to Indigenous Tasmanians, and the skull has often been cited in debates about the ancestors of Aboriginal Australians and Papuans; modern researchers caution that a single skull says little about population history, and genetic evidence is now usually used instead.'),
  ('Rainforest as a barrier?','The Niah evidence for early rainforest foraging, including the use of fire and plant processing, challenged an older idea that tropical rainforests were too hard for early humans to live in.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.jhevol.2006.08.011','wiki:Niah_National_Park'],img='Niah National Park',dateNote='Deep Skull about 40,000 years old; found 1958',
 changed="1958: found and dated to about 40,000 years; later decades: questions over its context; 2000-2004 re-excavation and the 2007 publication reaffirmed an early modern human presence."),
X('bluefish-caves','Bluefish Caves: people in the Yukon 24,000 years ago?',H,'disputed',2,-22000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Bluefish Caves are three small caves in the northern Yukon, Canada, about 54 km southwest of the Vuntut Gwitchin community of Old Crow. Archaeologist Jacques Cinq-Mars excavated them between 1977 and 1987 and argued that cut-marked animal bones showed people were there long before the Clovis culture; his claim met strong scepticism for decades. In 2017 Lauriane Bourgeon, Ariane Burke and Thomas Higham re-examined the bones and reported cut marks on a horse jaw and other remains dated to about 24,000 calibrated years ago. They argued this supported the 'Beringian standstill' idea that the ancestors of Native Americans lived isolated in Beringia during the Last Glacial Maximum.",
 [('Questioned dates','A later critique argued that the stratigraphic context was disturbed and that some marks could be natural; Bourgeon defended the results, and the site remains contested.'),
  ('Part of a wider shift','Bluefish Caves is one of several sites, together with White Sands in New Mexico and Monte Verde in Chile, cited by researchers who argue that the Americas were peopled earlier than the "Clovis first" model allowed; other researchers still accept only somewhat later dates.')],
 ['doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0169486','wiki:Bluefish_Caves'],img=None,dateNote='Cut-marked bones dated to about 24,000 years ago (contested); excavated 1977-1987',statusNote='dates and human origin of marks disputed',
 changed="1977-1987: Cinq-Mars claims a very early date and is largely disbelieved; 2017: re-analysis revives the claim; debate continues."),
X('lonar-crater','Lonar Lake: an impact crater, a slain demon and temples on the rim',H,'confirmed',3,-50000,['geological','dating','textual'],
 "Lonar Lake in Buldhana district, Maharashtra, India, is a salty soda lake filling a crater about 1.8 km across and 150 m deep, blasted into the Deccan basalt by a meteorite impact. It is India's only crater lake of meteoritic origin, a National Geological Monument and a Ramsar wetland site. A British officer, C. J. E. Alexander, recorded the crater in 1823. Its age is uncertain: earlier analyses gave about 52,000 years, while argon-argon dating gave about 570,000 years, although that older figure has been disputed. Ancient temples stand around the lake.",
 [('Lonasura the demon','Local tradition says Vishnu slew the asura (demon) Lonasura here, giving Lonar its name, and the Daitya Sudan temple is linked to this story; descriptions in the Skanda Purana and the 16th-century Ain-i-Akbari have been associated with the lake, though the identification is not certain.'),
  ('Volcano or meteorite?','Before shocked minerals and impact glass were identified, some geologists thought the crater might be volcanic; the impact origin is now accepted.')],
 ['wiki:Lonar_Lake'],img='Lonar Lake',dateNote='Impact about 52,000 or 570,000 years ago (disputed); recorded 1823'),
]
# --- part 2: South and Southeast Asia
E+=[
X('keezhadi','Keezhadi: a Sangam-age town on the Vaigai and an argument over dates and scripts',H,'confirmed',2,-580,['archaeological','radiocarbon','epigraphic'],
 "Keezhadi (Keeladi) is an ancient settlement site about 12 km southeast of Madurai in Tamil Nadu, India, on the bank of the Vaigai River. An Archaeological Survey of India team led by Amarnath Ramakrishnan began digging there in June 2015, and the Tamil Nadu State Department of Archaeology has led the work since 2019. Excavators have found brick walls, roof tiles, pottery, bone tools, copper needles, iron objects and many potsherds inscribed in Tamil-Brahmi script, the early writing used for Old Tamil. AMS radiocarbon dates on charcoal from the fourth season reached back to about 580 BCE, and the site was occupied until about the 1st century CE. Keezhadi is widely presented as evidence of urban life in Tamil country during or before the Sangam period described in early Tamil poetry.",
 [('How old is Tamil-Brahmi writing?','Supporters of an early date argue that Keezhadi pushes Tamil-Brahmi writing back to the 6th century BCE, earlier than the Ashokan Brahmi inscriptions of the 3rd century BCE. Critics, including a central government minister in 2019, say the inscribed sherds came from mixed, disturbed layers such as an ancient rubbish pit, so they cannot be securely tied to the dated charcoal.'),
  ('An Indus link?','Excavators noted graffiti marks on some pottery and compared them with Indus-script signs, and some officials suggested a link between Indus writing and Tamil-Brahmi; most specialists regard such links as unproven, since the Indus script is undeciphered and the sites are separated by more than a thousand years.')],
 ['wiki:Keezhadi_excavation_site'],img='File:KeeladiExcavationCamp2.jpg',end=100,dateNote='Occupation about 6th century BCE to 1st century CE (dates debated); excavated since 2015',statusNote='site confirmed; earliest dates for writing disputed'),
X('sinauli-carts','Sinauli: Bronze Age coffins, swords and "chariots" in the Ganges plain',H,'disputed',2,-1800,['archaeological'],
 "Sinauli is an archaeological site in the Ganga-Yamuna Doab of western Uttar Pradesh, India, found by chance when farmers were levelling land. Archaeological Survey of India excavations in 2005-2006 uncovered a cemetery of more than a hundred burials of people of all ages. Trial excavations in March-May 2018, about 100 m away, found coffin burials, copper helmets and antenna-hilted swords, and three full-sized wooden vehicles with solid disc wheels covered in copper sheets. The finds are dated to roughly 2000-1500 BCE and attributed to the Ochre Coloured Pottery / Copper Hoard culture, which existed at the same time as the Late Harappan phase of the Indus civilisation.",
 [('Warrior chariots and Vedic rites','The 2018 excavation director, Sanjay Manjul, presented the vehicles as chariots, possibly drawn by horses, and said the burial rituals showed close affinity with Vedic rituals; some media and commentators linked them to the warrior world of the Mahabharata.'),
  ('Carts, not chariots','Many archaeologists point out that solid-wheeled vehicles are carts, not the light spoked-wheel chariots associated with horses. Asko Parpola argued they were ox-drawn carts and connected the burials to an early wave of Indo-Iranian speakers. Others reject that link, and the attribution of the earlier cemetery to the Harappan civilisation proper is also contested.')],
 ['wiki:Sinauli'],img='File:Sinauli Chariot.jpg',end=-1500,dateNote='About 2000-1500 BCE; excavated 2005-2006 and 2018',statusNote='finds confirmed; "chariot" interpretation disputed'),
X('taxila','Taxila: crossroads city of Gandhara, Alexander and the Buddhists',H,'confirmed',2,-800,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Taxila (Takshashila) in Rawalpindi District, Punjab, Pakistan, is a cluster of ancient city sites at the junction of South and Central Asia and one of the capitals of Gandhara. The oldest city mound, Bhir Mound, dates from around the 8th to 6th centuries BCE. Taxila may have been the capital of the Achaemenid Persian province of Hindush, and its king, called Taxiles by the Greeks, submitted to Alexander the Great in 326 BCE. Buddhist texts say Ashoka governed it before becoming emperor and that Chanakya, adviser to Chandragupta Maurya, came from there. Indo-Greeks built the new city of Sirkap, and Indo-Scythians, Indo-Parthians and Kushans later ruled. The Chinese pilgrim Faxian visited around 400 CE, and Huna invaders devastated the region in the 5th century. Alexander Cunningham identified the ruins in the 19th century, John Marshall excavated them for about twenty years from 1913, and UNESCO listed Taxila in 1980.",
 [('The first university?','Taxila is often called one of the world\'s earliest universities, and texts describe students travelling there to study under teachers; many historians stress that, unlike Nalanda, there is no archaeological evidence for a single organised institution with halls and a library, so "university" may overstate it.'),
  ('Legends in place-names','Local tradition linked Sirkap ("cut-off head") to Buddhist stories in which the Buddha gave his head as an act of charity in a former life; such names show how Buddhist legend was mapped onto the landscape.')],
 ['wiki:Taxila'],img='Taxila',end=500,dateNote='About 800 BCE to the 5th century CE; UNESCO 1980'),
X('muziris-pattanam','Muziris: Rome\'s pepper port in Kerala and the search for it at Pattanam',H,'confirmed',2,-100,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Muziris (Tamil Muciri) was an ancient port on the Malabar Coast of southern India, in the Chera country, near the mouth of the Periyar River in modern Kerala. Early Tamil Sangam poems describe sacks of pepper piled up and gold arriving by ship. Greek and Roman sources, including the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea and Pliny the Elder, who called it 'the first emporium of India', describe it as a hub of the Indian Ocean spice trade. A 2nd-century CE Greek papyrus records a loan contract between merchants for a cargo shipped from Muziris to Alexandria in Egypt. Its location was long assumed to be near Kodungallur. Excavations from 2007 at Pattanam by the Kerala Council for Historical Research found Roman amphora fragments, glass and other imports, and some experts now favour that site.",
 [('Is Pattanam really Muziris?','The excavators and the Kerala government promoted Pattanam as the lost port, but some historians dispute the identification and the way the finds have been interpreted, arguing that the evidence shows a trading settlement without proving it was Muziris itself.'),
  ('Lost to a flood','Muziris is usually said to have vanished after a great flood of the Periyar in 1341 that reshaped the coast and opened the present harbour at Kochi. Historians Rajan Gurukkal and Dick Whittaker argue that this was only the most dramatic of many gradual changes in the landscape.')],
 ['wiki:Muziris'],img='File:Amphorae sherds, Pattanam excavation site (Kerala).jpg',end=1341,dateNote='Peak of Roman trade about 1st century BCE to 2nd century CE; Pattanam excavated from 2007'),
X('brihadisvara-temple','The Big Temple of Thanjavur: Rajaraja Chola\'s granite mountain',H,'confirmed',2,1003,['architectural','epigraphic'],
 "The Brihadisvara Temple (Rajarajeshvaram, the 'Big Temple') in Thanjavur, Tamil Nadu, is a Shaivite Hindu temple built by the Chola emperor Rajaraja I between 1003 and 1010 CE at the height of Chola power. Its granite vimana (tower) above the shrine is one of the tallest in South India, at about 66 m, and its top is described as a square granite block weighing around 80 tonnes. The walls carry long inscriptions recording donations, temple staff and land grants, which make it an important historical archive. With the Chola temples of Gangaikonda Cholapuram and Darasuram (Airavatesvara) it forms the UNESCO World Heritage Site of the Great Living Chola Temples.",
 [('The shadowless tower','A popular claim, repeated in tourist literature and online, says the tower\'s shadow never falls on the ground at noon. Any tall object casts a shadow, and close to the tropics the noon sun is high for part of the year, so the shadow is then short and falls mostly on the temple\'s own base. There is no evidence the builders designed this effect or recorded it.'),
  ('A monument of empire','Historians read the temple, its name ("the lord of Rajaraja") and its inscriptions, including one of 1011 CE naming the people employed by the temple with their roles and wages, as a statement of Rajaraja\'s imperial power as well as an act of devotion; how far it was planned as royal propaganda is a matter of interpretation.')],
 ['wiki:Brihadisvara_Temple'],img='Brihadisvara Temple',dateNote='Built 1003-1010 CE',statusNote='temple confirmed; popular "no-shadow" claim is a myth'),
X('bhangarh-fort','Bhangarh Fort: India\'s "most haunted" ruin and its two curses',M,'myth-legend',3,1573,['oral tradition','architectural'],
 "Bhangarh is a ruined 16th-century fort town on the edge of the Sariska tiger reserve in the Aravalli hills of Alwar district, Rajasthan, India. It was established under the Kachhwaha ruler Bhagwant Das as the residence of his son Madho Singh. Its temples, palace, havelis and bazaar street are well preserved, but the town was later abandoned. It is now a protected monument of the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) and is often called the most haunted place in India.",
 [('The sadhu\'s shadow','One legend says a holy man living in the precinct (named in some versions as Guru Balu Nath) allowed the town to be built on condition that no house should cast a shadow on his own. When a raised palace did so, the town was destroyed.'),
  ('The tantric and the princess','Another tale says a priest who practised black magic fell for a beautiful princess (named Ratnavati in popular versions) and gave her an enchanted potion. She threw it onto a rock, which rolled onto him and crushed him, and as he died he cursed the town.'),
  ('A notice, not a ghost certificate','An ASI signboard at the entrance prohibits entry between sunset and sunrise and is often cited as official proof of a haunting. Sceptics and travel writers point out that the board does not mention ghosts and that dusk-to-dawn closure is a normal rule for unlit, unguarded ruins near wildlife. There is no evidence of paranormal activity.')],
 ['wiki:Bhangarh_Fort','wiki:Bhangarh','url:https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/bhangarh-fort|Bhangarh Fort (Atlas Obscura)|magazine'],img='File:Notice board at Bhangarh Fort.jpg',dateNote='Town founded 1573; legends of later origin'),
X('jatinga-birds','Jatinga: the hill village where birds "commit suicide"',H,'confirmed',3,1960,['observational','ornithological'],
 "Jatinga is a village of about 2,500 people, mostly Khasi with a Dimasa minority, on a ridge in Dima Hasao district, Assam, India. At the end of the monsoon season, roughly September to November, birds of many species become disoriented on dark, foggy, moonless evenings. They fly towards the village lights, where many are caught or killed. The tea planter and naturalist E. P. Gee, who visited with the ornithologist Salim Ali, brought the phenomenon to international attention in the 1960s, and it has since been nicknamed 'the Bermuda Triangle of birds'.",
 [('Not suicide','Ornithologists explain the deaths as disorientation, not suicide. Local and migrating birds disturbed by fog and strong winds at altitude, and lacking moonlight to navigate by, are drawn to artificial lights and then trapped or killed by people. Some villagers have deliberately used torches and lamps to lure them.'),
  ('Mystery versus explanation','The event is still promoted as a mystery, but the main open questions are scientific ones, such as which weather conditions and species are involved. Conservationists have encouraged pig farming as an alternative to eating the trapped birds, and bird-centred tourism has grown since the 1980s.')],
 ['wiki:Jatinga'],img='File:View of Jatinga near Haflong,Dima Hasao.jpg',dateNote='Seasonal phenomenon publicised in the 1960s; recurs each autumn',statusNote='bird deaths confirmed; "suicide" is a myth'),
X('lac-long-quan-au-co','Lạc Long Quân and Âu Cơ: the dragon, the mountain fairy and a hundred eggs',M,'myth-legend',2,-2879,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "In the Vietnamese origin myth, Lạc Long Quân, the 'Dragon Lord of Lạc', married Âu Cơ, a mountain fairy or goddess. She gave birth to a sac of 100 eggs, which hatched into 100 children. Saying 'I am descended from dragons, you from immortals; we are as incompatible as water and fire', the couple parted. Fifty children went with their father to the sea and fifty with their mother to the mountains. The eldest son became the first Hùng king, ruling the kingdom of Văn Lang from Phong Châu in present-day Phú Thọ province. The 15th-century chronicle Đại Việt sử ký toàn thư dates the Hồng Bàng period from 2879 BCE to the conquest of Văn Lang by An Dương Vương in 258 BCE. The Hùng Kings' commemoration day is a national holiday in Vietnam.",
 [('Myth as national charter','Historians read the story as a charter for Vietnamese identity. The Vietnamese word đồng bào ("compatriot") literally means "from the same womb", echoing the shared egg sac, and the split between sea and mountains may reflect the country\'s coastal lowlanders and highland peoples.'),
  ('Legend and archaeology','Many scholars regard the Hùng kings as legendary or semi-legendary, with a long reign list and dates set down much later. Others link the tradition to the real Bronze Age Đông Sơn culture of the Red River region, which flourished in the last centuries BCE, while noting that the 2879 BCE date cannot be confirmed.')],
 ['wiki:Lạc_Long_Quân','wiki:Hồng_Bàng_dynasty','wiki:Hùng_king'],img='Lạc Long Quân',end=-258,dateNote='Traditional dates 2879-258 BCE from a 15th-century chronicle'),
X('kuntilanak-pontianak','The kuntilanak (pontianak): the white-clad ghost who named a city',M,'myth-legend',3,1771,['oral tradition','folklore'],
 "The kuntilanak (Indonesian) or pontianak (Malay) is a vengeful female ghost of Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore, usually said to be the spirit of a woman who died in childbirth or linked to the stillborn child of the langsuir, another female ghost. She is often pictured as a beautiful long-haired woman in white who reveals a monstrous form, with long nails or fangs, to lure and attack men. Her presence is signalled by an infant's cry or the scent of frangipani (plumeria) mixed with the smell of decay. The city of Pontianak in West Kalimantan, Borneo, founded in 1771 by Syarif Abdurrahman Alkadrie, is traditionally said to take its name from her: the legend says the site was haunted by these ghosts until he drove them away with cannon fire.",
 [('A widespread type','The kuntilanak can be compared with other ghosts of women who died in pregnancy or childbirth, such as the churel of South Asia and La Llorona of Latin America, and see in these figures recurring anxieties about childbirth, death and women wronged by men.'),
  ('Folk protection and film','Folklore holds that driving a nail into the hole at the nape of her neck turns the pontianak into a beautiful woman and good wife until the nail is removed. Malaysian studios made a famous series of pontianak films from 1957, beginning with Cathay-Keris\'s Pontianak, and she remains a staple of Indonesian and Malaysian horror.')],
 ['wiki:Kuntilanak'],img=None,dateNote='Folklore of uncertain age; linked to the founding of Pontianak city in 1771'),
X('bujang-valley','Bujang Valley: iron, temples and Southeast Asia\'s oldest brick monument',H,'confirmed',2,110,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "The Bujang Valley (Lembah Bujang) near Merbok in Kedah, Malaysia, between Mount Jerai and the Muda River, is the richest archaeological area in Malaysia. It belonged to the old kingdom of Kedah, an Indianised Hindu-Buddhist trading state on the route between India and China. More than 50 brick temples (candi) of about the 5th to 14th centuries CE have been excavated. At Sungai Batu, excavators from the Centre for Global Archaeological Research at Universiti Sains Malaysia, led by Mohd Mokhtar Saidin since 2008, have found jetty remains, iron-smelting sites and a clay-brick monument dated to about 110 CE, which they describe as the oldest recorded man-made structure in Southeast Asia. Since the extended site was recognised it has been proposed for the UNESCO World Heritage List.",
 [('How early, and whose?','The early Sungai Batu dates and the claim of the oldest monument in the region are debated by some archaeologists. So is the balance between local initiative and Indian influence in the rise of old Kedah.'),
  ('Heritage under threat','In 2013 a land developer demolished Candi No. 11, a temple about 1,200 years old, prompting outrage in Malaysia and abroad. The case highlighted how quickly unprotected sites can be lost.')],
 ['wiki:Bujang_Valley'],img='Bujang Valley',end=1400,dateNote='About 110 CE (earliest claimed) to 14th century'),
X('mahendraparvata-lidar','Mahendraparvata: the Khmer city lidar found under the forest',H,'confirmed',2,802,['lidar survey','archaeological','epigraphic'],
 "Mahendraparvata ('mountain of the great Indra') is an early capital of the Khmer Empire on the Phnom Kulen plateau in Siem Reap Province, Cambodia, about 40 km north of Angkor Wat. According to later inscriptions, Jayavarman II was consecrated there as universal monarch in 802, the date traditionally taken as the founding of the Khmer Empire. Scattered temples on Kulen had long been known, but the city plan lay hidden under forest. In 2012 an expedition led by Damian Evans and Jean-Baptiste Chevance used helicopter-mounted lidar (airborne laser scanning) to reveal a grid of streets, embankments, ponds and temple mounds. The results were announced in June 2013 and published with the wider Angkor survey in PNAS.",
 [('Why abandon it?','Evans has suggested that deforestation and water-management problems contributed to the decline of the Kulen city, as they may later have done at Angkor; others place more weight on political shifts, and the causes are still being studied.'),
  ('Lost cities and the jungle myth','The discovery was widely reported as a "lost city found", although Kulen\'s temples, and its importance in Khmer tradition, were never forgotten locally; archaeologists stress that lidar mainly reveals the scale and layout of urban landscapes already partly known.')],
 ['doi:10.1073/pnas.1306539110','wiki:Mahendraparvata'],img='File:Phnom Kulen8.JPG',dateNote='Consecration of Jayavarman II traditionally 802 CE; lidar survey 2012'),
]
# --- part 3: Oceania and the Americas
E+=[
X('great-american-interchange','The Great American Interchange: when the Isthmus of Panama joined two worlds',H,'confirmed',2,-2700000,['fossil','geological','genetic'],
 "For tens of millions of years South America was an island continent with its own animals: marsupial-like predators such as the sabre-toothed Thylacosmilus, giant ground sloths, armoured glyptodonts, hoofed 'southern ungulates' and flightless 'terror birds'. As the volcanic Isthmus of Panama rose from the sea, a land bridge formed and animals crossed in both directions, with migration accelerating dramatically about 2.7 million years ago. Armadillos, opossums and porcupines spread north, while cats, dogs, bears, horses, camels and deer moved south. The joining also split the Atlantic from the Pacific, a separation sometimes called the Great American Schism, which led to diversification and extinction in the seas on either side.",
 [('When did the isthmus close?','In 2015 Camilo Montes and colleagues argued from Colombian rocks that the seaway had effectively closed by about 13-15 million years ago; Aaron O\'Dea and a large team replied in 2016 that the marine, fossil and genetic evidence still points to final closure around 2.8 million years ago. Both sides agree that some animals crossed earlier by island-hopping or rafting.'),
  ('Why did northerners do better?','North American mammals that went south were, on the whole, more successful than southern ones going north, and many native South American groups went extinct; proposed explanations include competition, climate and the larger area of North America, and the debate is ongoing.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/sciadv.1600883','wiki:Great_American_Interchange'],img='Great American Interchange',dateNote='Main exchange from about 2.7 million years ago; isthmus closure date debated'),
X('spirit-cave-mummy','The Spirit Cave mummy: North America\'s oldest mummy and the right to rebury',H,'confirmed',2,-9500,['archaeological','radiocarbon','genetic'],
 "The Spirit Cave mummy is the naturally mummified body of a man buried wrapped in finely woven mats and a rabbit-skin robe in a small cave about 21 km east of Fallon, Nevada. It was excavated in 1940 by Sydney and Georgia Wheeler and taken to the Nevada State Museum, where it was assumed to be 1,500-2,000 years old. In 1996 accelerator mass spectrometry radiocarbon dating showed it was about 11,500 years old (roughly 9,400 radiocarbon years), making it the oldest known mummy in North America. The Fallon Paiute-Shoshone Tribe claimed it under the Native American Graves Protection and Repatriation Act (NAGPRA) in 1997, which began a long legal dispute. In 2015 the tribe allowed the Danish geneticist Eske Willerslev to sample it. Results published in 2018 showed the man was closely related to Indigenous peoples of the Americas, and the tribe reburied him in 2018.",
 [('Who were the first Americans?','In the 1990s some studies of skull shape suggested that very early individuals such as Spirit Cave and Kennewick Man looked different from modern Native Americans, and this was taken up in speculation about earlier non-Native settlers. Genome studies of both have since found them closely related to present-day Indigenous Americans, and most researchers now treat skull shape as a poor guide to ancestry.'),
  ('Science and sovereignty','The case is often cited, with Kennewick Man, in debates over how museums and scientists should work with descendant communities; some saw the long dispute as a barrier to research, others as a necessary assertion of Indigenous rights that ended in a collaborative study.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.aav2621','wiki:Spirit_Cave_mummy'],img='Spirit Cave mummy',dateNote='About 11,500 years ago; found 1940, dated 1996, reburied 2018',
 changed="1940: thought to be about 2,000 years old; 1996: re-dated to about 11,500 years; 1997-2016: repatriation dispute; 2018: genome published and remains reburied."),
X('etowah-mounds','Etowah: copper "birdmen" and painted marble ancestors of a Mississippian town',H,'confirmed',2,1000,['archaeological'],
 "Etowah Indian Mounds is a 54-acre archaeological site on the Etowah River near Cartersville, Bartow County, Georgia, United States, built and occupied in phases between about 1000 and 1550 CE. It is regarded as one of the most intact Mississippian culture sites in the Southeast, with large flat-topped platform mounds, a plaza and defensive ditches. Elite burials contained embossed copper plates, including figures of winged 'birdman' dancers, shell gorgets, and textiles preserved by contact with copper, as well as symbols of the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex such as the hand-and-eye and the solar cross. A pair of painted marble statues of a seated man and a kneeling woman is among the best-known finds. Cyrus Thomas and John P. Rogan tested the site for the Smithsonian Institution in 1883. The Muscogee Creek and Cherokee peoples, who each lived in the area at different times, regard it as sacred.",
 [('Who built the mounds?','In the 19th century some writers attributed eastern North American mounds to a vanished non-Native race of "Mound Builders"; the Smithsonian survey that tested Etowah in the 1880s helped show that the ancestors of Native Americans built them, and this is the mainstream view.'),
  ('Birdman meanings','The falcon-costumed figures are interpreted variously as warrior dancers, a mythic hero sometimes linked to the Morning Star, or ancestral beings of the upper world; these readings draw on later Native oral traditions and remain interpretations.')],
 ['wiki:Etowah_Indian_Mounds'],img='Etowah Indian Mounds',end=1550,dateNote='About 1000-1550 CE'),
X('caracol-maya','Caracol: the Maya city that defeated Tikal and was revealed by lidar',H,'confirmed',2,562,['archaeological','epigraphic','lidar survey'],
 "Caracol is a large ancient Maya city on the Vaca Plateau in the Cayo District of Belize, in the foothills of the Maya Mountains. Its largest building, Caana ('sky palace'), at 43 m, is still the tallest man-made structure in Belize. Caracol was at first a client of the much more powerful Tikal, 76 km to the northwest, but inscriptions record that its ruler Lord Water (Yajaw Te' K'inich II) defeated Tikal's king Wak Chan K'awiil in a 'star war' in 562 CE, after which Caracol grew rapidly. The Caracol Archaeological Project, directed by Arlen and Diane Chase since 1985, used an aircraft-based lidar survey in 2009 to map about 200 km2 of terraces, causeways and houses under the forest, showing that the city covered an area larger than modern Belize City.",
 [('Who really beat Tikal?','Some epigraphers argue that the great power of Calakmul, Tikal\'s rival, was behind the 562 victory and that Caracol acted as its ally; how far Caracol acted alone is debated.'),
  ('Garden cities','The lidar maps support the idea that Classic Maya cities were low-density "garden cities" with houses spread among agricultural terraces, rather than compact towns surrounded by empty forest; the population estimates derived from such maps remain debated.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.jas.2010.09.018','wiki:Caracol'],img='Caracol',dateNote='"Star war" against Tikal 562 CE; area occupied from about 1200 BCE; final abandonment about 1050 CE; lidar survey 2009'),
X('ciudad-perdida','Ciudad Perdida (Teyuna): the "lost city" the Kogi never lost',H,'confirmed',2,800,['archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "Ciudad Perdida ('lost city'), also called Teyuna or Buritaca-200, is the ruins of a town in the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta of northern Colombia, built by the Tairona people and believed to have been founded around 800 CE, centuries before Machu Picchu. It consists of about 169 terraces carved into the mountainside, a network of stone-paved paths and small circular plazas, reached by about 1,200 stone steps through the forest. Local looters known as Los Sepúlvedas found it in 1972, and violence among looters alerted the authorities. A team from the Instituto Colombiano de Antropología reached the site in 1976 and it was restored between 1976 and 1982.",
 [('Lost to whom?','The Kogi, Arhuaco and Wiwa peoples, who regard themselves as descendants of the Tairona, have said they visited the site long before 1972 and kept it secret; for them Teyuna was never lost, and they continue to take part in its management.'),
  ('Conflict and tourism','In 2003 the ELN guerrilla group kidnapped eight foreign tourists there; since then the multi-day trek has become a major tourist attraction, raising debates about its impact on the sacred landscape.')],
 ['wiki:Ciudad_Perdida'],img='Ciudad Perdida',dateNote='Founded about 800 CE; found by looters 1972; excavated from 1976'),
X('canudos-war','Canudos: a prophet\'s town in the backlands destroyed by the Brazilian Republic',H,'confirmed',2,1896,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Antônio Conselheiro ('the Counsellor'), a wandering lay preacher, from about 1874 drew followers among the poor of the drought-stricken sertão of northeastern Brazil. In 1893 they founded the village of Canudos, which they called Belo Monte, in the interior of Bahia, and as the conflict went on it grew to tens of thousands of people (over 30,000 by some estimates). The new Brazilian Republic, proclaimed in 1889, came to see the settlement as a monarchist threat. Between 1896 and 1897 three military expeditions were defeated, including one led by Colonel Moreira César, who was killed. In October 1897 a fourth expedition under General Arthur Oscar bombarded and overran the town, razed it and killed nearly all its remaining inhabitants. Euclides da Cunha's book Os Sertões (1902) estimated about 30,000 dead in total, and other estimates vary.",
 [('Fanatics or a community of the poor?','The press and government portrayed the Conselheiristas as monarchist fanatics, and Euclides da Cunha, a journalist with the army, described them through the race theories of his time while also condemning the massacre; later historians often see Canudos as a self-governing refuge for the rural poor and former slaves that alarmed landowners and the Church.'),
  ('The hidden king','Conselheiro was said to prophesy the return of King Sebastian of Portugal, who vanished in battle in Morocco in 1578 and was expected to return as a saviour (Sebastianism); how central such prophecies were to life at Canudos is debated.')],
 ['wiki:War_of_Canudos','wiki:Antônio_Conselheiro'],img='War of Canudos',end=1897,dateNote='Settlement founded 1893; war 1896-1897'),
X('fordlandia','Fordlândia: Henry Ford\'s American town in the Amazon',H,'confirmed',3,1928,['documentary','textual'],
 "In 1928 the American industrialist Henry Ford obtained a concession of about 10,000 km2 on the Tapajós River in Pará, Brazil, to grow rubber for the Ford Motor Company, and built a prefabricated Midwestern-style town for 10,000 people with American-style houses, a hospital, a school, a swimming pool, a golf course and a cinema. The project struggled from the start. Rubber trees planted close together in plantations fell prey to blight, ants and insects that do less damage to scattered wild trees. Workers resented imposed American diets, alcohol bans and rules, and rioted in the cafeteria in 1930. Ford moved the plantation to Belterra in 1934. In 1945, as synthetic rubber reduced demand, Henry Ford II sold both sites back to the Brazilian government at a loss of more than US$20 million. Despite many invitations, Ford himself never visited either town.",
 [('Hubris or bad luck?','Historian Greg Grandin\'s 2009 book Fordlandia presents it as a parable of Ford\'s belief that American industrial and moral order could be transplanted anywhere; others emphasise more practical causes, such as inexperience with tropical agriculture and the collapse of rubber prices.'),
  ('Ghost town','Fordlândia is often described as an abandoned "ghost town", but it never fully emptied; about 90 residents remained for decades, and by 2017 the district had around 3,000 people.')],
 ['wiki:Fordlândia'],img='Fordlândia',end=1945,dateNote='Founded 1928; abandoned by Ford 1934; sold 1945'),
X('batavia-wreck-1629','The Batavia shipwreck and massacre, 1629',H,'confirmed',2,1629,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Batavia, a new flagship of the Dutch East India Company (VOC), was wrecked on 4 June 1629 on the Houtman Abrolhos islands off Western Australia on her maiden voyage to Batavia (Jakarta). About 300 of the 341 people aboard reached the islands. The commander, Francisco Pelsaert, took an open longboat nearly 3,000 km in 33 days to Batavia for help. He left the VOC official Jeronimus Cornelisz in charge, unaware that Cornelisz had been plotting mutiny. Cornelisz sent about 20 soldiers under Wiebbe Hayes to a nearby island to look for water and left them to die, then led a massacre over several weeks that killed about 125 survivors, including women and children. Hayes's group found water, built a small fort from limestone and coral blocks, and fought the mutineers until Pelsaert returned. Cornelisz and six others were tried and hanged, the first legal executions of Europeans in Australia. A lobster fisherman sighted the wreck in 1963, and archaeologists have since raised parts of the hull and, as recently as 2015, found skeletons of victims on Beacon Island.",
 [('The first European Australians?','Two lesser offenders, the soldier Wouter Loos and the cabin boy Jan Pelgrom de Bye, were marooned on the mainland and never heard of again, making them the first Europeans known to have stayed permanently on the continent. Writers have speculated about whether they lived among Aboriginal people, but there is no confirmed evidence of what happened to them.'),
  ('An alternative founding story','Because it took place 159 years before the First Fleet reached Sydney, the Batavia story is sometimes offered as an alternative founding narrative for European Australia; historians debate how much weight a wreck and its castaways should carry in national history.')],
 ['wiki:Batavia_(1628_ship)'],img='File:De BATAVIA voor de film onderweg als de NIEUW HOORN (02).jpg',dateNote='Wrecked 4 June 1629'),
X('leluh-kosrae','Leluh: the basalt-walled royal city of Kosrae',H,'confirmed',2,1300,['archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "Leluh is a ruined city on Lelu Island, a small island off Kosrae in the Federated States of Micronesia. Built of coral and basalt blocks, it reached its height around the 14th and 15th centuries as the capital of rulers who gradually unified Kosrae under a monarchy that archaeologists compare with those of Tonga or Hawaiʻi. The king and high aristocracy lived in the centre behind high basalt walls similar to those of Nan Madol on nearby Pohnpei, lesser nobles in coral houses to the west and commoners in simple huts. There were also royal tombs and sacred spaces. Parts of the city were still in use when Europeans arrived in the early 19th century, but Kosrae's population of about 6,000 fell to about 200 by 1870, and many stones were later reused. The site was listed on the US National Register of Historic Places in 1983.",
 [('Links with Nan Madol','Oral traditions on Pohnpei say the Saudeleur dynasty of Nan Madol was overthrown by Isokelekel, a hero from Kosrae; archaeologists see the similar basalt architecture as a sign of contact and competition between the two island states, though the details of the traditions cannot be checked independently.'),
  ('Built by giants or outsiders?','As at Nan Madol, the scale of the stonework has inspired claims of lost continents or outside builders; archaeological dating and local traditions point to construction by Kosraeans themselves.')],
 ['wiki:Leluh_archaeological_site','wiki:Lelu_Island'],img='Leluh archaeological site',dateNote='Peak about 14th-15th centuries CE'),
X('drop-bear','The drop bear: Australia\'s favourite hoax animal',M,'debunked',3,1967,['folklore','documentary'],
 "The drop bear is an imaginary predatory koala-like marsupial of Australian folklore. Tourists and newcomers are told that it lurks in trees and drops onto the heads of people walking beneath. Its origin is unknown. A 1967 article in the Australian Army newspaper Army mentioned 'a dreaded Drop Bear', and a 1976 article about an army base referred to legends of drop bears and hoop snakes said to have originated there. Recommended defences, all part of the joke, include forks in the hair, Vegemite or toothpaste behind the ears and speaking with an Australian accent. The Australian Museum keeps a tongue-in-cheek 'drop bear' entry written like a real species account, and Australian Geographic ran an April Fools' article about drop bears in 2013.",
 [('A memory of Thylacoleo?','Some observers have noted the drop bear\'s resemblance to Thylacoleo, the Pleistocene marsupial lion, a predator that may have climbed trees and was related to wombats and koalas. That likeness is a coincidence that people enjoy pointing out; there is no evidence that the joke preserves a memory of the extinct animal.'),
  ('Joke as social glue','Drop-bear stories work as an in-joke that frightens and confuses outsiders while amusing locals, like the North American "fearsome critters" such as the jackalope.')],
 ['wiki:Drop_bear','url:https://australian.museum/learn/animals/mammals/drop-bear/|Drop bear (Australian Museum)|museum'],img=None,dateNote='In print by 1967; origin unknown',statusNote='hoax / tall tale'),
]
